WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.680
Whereas a clustered index is based on a primary&nbsp;
key field, a nonclustered index can be based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.080
any other field. Nonclustered indexes are used&nbsp;
to speed up queries, but this comes at a cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.800
as each nonclustered index adds another way to&nbsp;
store information in a table, which adds size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.400
to the table. Take a look at this BikeRepairs&nbsp;
table. Which column, besides the BikeRepairID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.400
is the most likely to be used as criteria in&nbsp;
a query? The CustomerID field is very likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.560
so let's index that column. I have an INDEX&nbsp;
statement open here, and as you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.880
creating a nonclustered index takes little&nbsp;
text. First, we have a CREATE INDEX command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:48.880
and a name. Then, the keyword ON is used to get us&nbsp;
to the table on which this index will be created.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.320
Then in parentheses, we have the column&nbsp;
being indexed. I will execute this statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:05.760
and just like that, we have both a clustered and&nbsp;
nonclustered index in this BikeRepairs table.

